notes:
381,618,264 -> 155,285,258 (2.46x) from side-by-side block BANKS replacing the
one-block-per-row band policy. Room 44x118 -> 53x62, area2 16,641 -> 6,241.

THE FINDING: the band policy's premise was right and its conclusion wrong.
`r` binds whichever of ring_in/input is the nearer COLUMN, so there is one split
column — but NEITHER PIPE HAS TO STAND OVER THE CODE IT SERVES. Push the ring
pair as far east as it can go while staying west of the split, put `input` on the
east wall, and the split moves from the middle of the room to a few columns from
its east edge. RING then gets 3/4 of the width, which is the ratio snake's blocks
actually want (26 RING-only vs 7 IO-only). Spreading each pair symmetrically
(SPREAD=3) keeps both midpoints on the same column and leaves the painter's riser
a column of its own. snake_place.layout is 20 lines.

THE THREE LEVERS, measured independently in the band layout so area2 is pinned:
    DFS block_order                     20,311 ticks
    shipped tuned_order                 15,147
    MinLA anneal, unweighted            14,395   <- order: 1.05x over shipped
    loop-weighted x2 / x3 / x4 / x16    14,955 / 14,830 / 15,350 / 16,502
    hot/cold partition raw              21,621
    hot/cold partition + MinLA          14,403
  packing: 2.67x on area2. That is essentially the entire win.

Two negative results worth keeping:
 * STRUCTURAL LOOP WEIGHTING IS MONOTONICALLY WORSE — the same trap the
   public-case profile fell into earlier, for the same reason.
 * HOT/COLD PARTITION ADDS NOTHING (14,403 vs 14,395); MinLA already pushes the
   six depth-0 blocks to the margins. Raw partition without MinLA is worse than DFS.
 * AGAINST Z3: raising the anneal budget to 250k steps found a LOWER modelled
   MinLA cost that measured WORSE on the engine (14,820 vs 14,395). The objective
   is not a faithful proxy past a point, so an exact optimum buys nothing.

Two silent placer bugs, now tested: a corridor may cross a walked blank but never
TURN on one, and two holes let a corridor hijack a block's own man while the grid
still loaded — the padding a literal's backtick-skip leaves between digits, and
the run from a wrapped row's turn glyph to its first op that `seek` jumps but the
man still crosses. The second hijacked INIT on its second row: 2/13 frames wrong,
no error. Field.walked + blockplace.walk_edges catch both.
